{
  "image_name": "63ced0efeb481f6e7c8c47868c7fbddf.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several strategies for handling scaling and hardness in saline water treatment, especially around reverse osmosis (RO). The top row contrasts pretreatment approaches. One method uses anti-scalants to inhibit crystal nucleation so salts stay dissolved rather than precipitating on a membrane. Another intentionally adds chemicals so troublesome minerals precipitate out in a reactor before the brine continues downstream. A third approach passes raw brine through an ion-exchange resin, where mineral-forming ions are swapped to soften the brine. A more elaborate option uses salt-splitting electrodialysis with selective membranes to separate ions from a calcium sulfate brine into two different product streams, shown as calcium chloride and sodium sulfate.\n\nThe bottom row compares RO system configurations. Closed-circuit RO recirculates concentrate around the membrane module while producing fresh water and discharging brine. Batch RO adds a variable-volume pressurized tank and circulation loop, suggesting operation on a finite batch rather than continuous once-through flow. Flow-reversal RO alternates the direction of flow through the system using switches, apparently to redistribute concentration buildup and reduce fouling or scaling. Overall, the figure presents complementary chemical and process-engineering tools for controlling mineral precipitation in high-salinity water treatment.",
